I fell hopelessly in love 15 years ago I  was only 16 years old a junior in high  school but Stacey Carson was a mature  sophisticated 18 year old and as far as  I could tell she was absolutely perfect  drop dead gorgeous incredibly kind a ton  of fun to hang out with and she had a  way of making everyone she talked to  feel as though they were the most  special person in the world she had my  heart when the opportunity came to ask  her out I seize the moment a friend  called one night to tell me that she had  just split with her boyfriend so I put  on my oversized green suit and tie and I  sprayed on way too much cologne I  grabbed a rose for my mom's flower vase  and I jumped into my brother's red 1987  chevrolet camaro and i sped down to the  high school two blocks away arriving  just in time to see Stacey exiting the  school after finishing her after school  dance rehearsals I put my gasps my foot  to the gas and I sped across the parking  lot I can do a screeching halt in front  of Stacey no doubt she was terrified and  I swung open that big heavy door and  jumped and slid across the hood not  extended the rose  and then without even thinking the words  that came out of my mouth where will you  marry me I was shocked because this is  not what I wanted to say I was even more  surprised when she actually said yes  because that's who she was and then she  laughed and I laughed we laughed  together for a while and then I asked  her to attend the winter ball with me to  which he obliged I spent the next two  weeks doing everything I could to make  sure I would be the perfect day for her  including taking dance lessons because  she loved dancing and I was terrible at  it but that wasn't going to stop me and  the night of the dance came and it was  magical we danced the night away I was  falling in love five years later I asked  Stacy Carson that same big question this  time on purpose and she said this time  without laughing yes and on August  nineteenth 2006 at 1pm we were married  it was another magical moment we were in  love five years passed approaching our  fifth wedding anniversary and a lot had  changed I was working 80 plus hours per  week for a crazy consulting firm and my  wife was working even harder to take  care of our six month old baby boy Jake  we were both completely exhausted I came  home one night after a regular day in  the office 2 a.m. and i walked in to  find my poor wife sitting on the kitchen  floor with tears streaming down her  mascara stained face she looked at me  and she said why aren't you hear from  you when I need you and then without  even thinking the words that came out of  my mouth were I'm doing the best I can  for this family why aren't you more  appreciative and I went to bed that  night ashamed and asking myself the  question what happened to our hopeless  love is this a sign that it's not meant  to be I found the  these questions in the work of dr.  Jennifer finlayson fife a marriage and  family counselor from the University of  Boston from Boston University in her  work she teaches that love those through  three phases phase one love without  knowledge this is the blissfully in love  phase that we all enjoy when our first  falling in love this is the phase when  your partner is always on your mind and  you're willing to do anything for her or  him including taking dance lessons in  this phase your brain is actually  addicted to love your brain enjoys  heightened levels of neurotransmitters  adrenaline dopamine and serotonin all  which formed this crazy cocktail of love  that leaves you feeling joy  vulnerability and complete love so it  seems but this phase will not last  forever because eventually that  heightened level of euphoria goes away  and you enter phase two knowledge  without love this is the might as well  be roommates phase  in this phase reality sets in and you  start to notice the small things and the  big things about your partner that are  failing to meet your expectations and  you stop trying to impress this is the  phase you realize that love is hard this  is where I was on that fateful night at  2am but the beautiful thing is that love  can be better than that and those who  choose to leave that phase will enter  phase 3 knowledge with love in this  phase you still have a full appreciation  for your partner's weaknesses and faults  you still know exactly how your  relationship is failing to meet your  needs lets you choose not to dwell on  them instead you choose to focus on  improving yourself for your partner  because love is worth it and when the  two partners equally commit to working  on self-improvement they can grow in 2  phase 3 and at that point you can really  enjoy the fruits of long lasting love  and companionship Stacy and I have been  married 10 years this August and in a  few days we welcome our third child into  our family I love her and we are not  always in phase 3 but we know how to get  there and we know that it's worth it and  we're both committed to working toward  it every single day so I want to leave  you with a few tips that I've learned  through my 10 years of marriage which  doesn't make me an expert but also not a  novice I like to consider myself an  apprentice of love tip number one learn  how to love dr. Gary Chapman in his new  york times bestselling book the five  love languages teaches us that people  feel loved in different ways your love  language maybe physical touch whereas  yours maybe words of affirmation this  distinction does not matter in phase one  but it does in phase two I know that  stacy is  happy and feeling love when I speak a  specific dialect of physical touch the  daily foot massage I must have been high  on dopamine the day I committed to that  one advice number to learn how to fight  dr. John Gottman is the master at this  in his 30-plus year longitudinal study  he came to to I think relevant  conclusions one greater than sixty  percent of relationship conflict is  unresolvable it's simply a difference of  opinions and number two the marital  masters learn to fight with a smile on  their face I know this is easy to say  and I know it's a lot harder to do trust  me but I also know that it's worth it so  next time you're in a fight don't try to  be right try to understand your  partner's point of view try to argue  with compassion finally advice number  three learn how to forgive unfortunately  I have seen how an unwillingness to  forgive can tear apart families the  longer you're with someone the more  likely it is that feelings will be hurt  that's just the reality of love and if  we let that hurt feelings linger for too  long they will poison your ability to  live everyday expressing those love  languages to live every day arguing with  compassion they'll prevent you from  enjoying the true joys of long laughs  deep lasting relationships that I enjoy  every day with Stacey so that's it no  big secret that's how it works for me so  next time you find yourself in a  long-term relationship and things get  rocky because trust me I promise they  will rather than asking is this meant to  be first try asking what more can I do  and then get to work because it's hard  but I can tell you that it's worth it  thank  you
 

